  "problem": "The angle $\\theta =-{395}^{∘}$ is represented in the coordinate plane in the usual way. In which quadrant does it lie?",
  "worked_solution": "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $-{395}^{∘}$ by *adding* integer multiples of ${360}^{∘}$ until we get an angle that lies in the range $[{0}^{∘},{360}^{∘}).$\n\n- $-{395}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}=-{35}^{∘}$\n\n- $-{395}^{∘}+2\\cdot {360}^{∘}={325}^{∘}\\,✓$\n\nSo the angle ${325}^{∘}$ is coterminal with $-{395}^{∘}.$ Let's draw this coterminal angle in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14223930/assets/936e222cebb005bc504867f26eb2144bb976cab2441e53051121e056be158be1.png)\n\nWe see that ${325}^{∘}$ lies in quadrant IV. Therefore, $-{395}^{∘}$ also lies in this quadrant.",
